  <section class="section-block">
    <h2>FinTech and digital finance: the fastest-growing branch of commerce careers</h2>
    <p class="line">
      India's FinTech industry is projected to reach $1.5 trillion in 2025, with transaction revenues expected to hit $50 billion by 2030.
    </p>
    <p class="line">
      UPI now handles 81% of all retail digital payments in India, with over 20 billion UPI transactions recorded in February 2026 alone.
    </p>
    <p class="line">
      That scale creates real hiring demand, and commerce graduates are well-placed to fill it because these roles mix finance understanding with tech and process skills.
    </p>
    <div class="grid-two">
      <div class="card">
        <h3>FinTech roles a commerce graduate can realistically enter</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>FinTech Analyst:</strong> Studying market trends, product viability, and competitor benchmarking for digital finance companies. Entry salary ₹4–7 LPA.</li>
          <li><strong>Digital Banking Executive:</strong> Supporting UPI, mobile banking operations, and customer onboarding at banks and neo-banking platforms.</li>
          <li><strong>Payment Operations Specialist:</strong> Managing reconciliation, settlements, and dispute resolution in payment gateways and wallets.</li>
          <li><strong>Compliance and Regulatory Associate:</strong> Working on KYC/AML, RBI compliance, and risk frameworks at fintech startups.</li>
          <li><strong>FinTech Product Support:</strong> Bridging user feedback, operations, and product teams at lending or investment platforms.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>What this path needs and what it does not</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>You do not need a coding background. Many roles in fintech compliance, operations, and analysis do not require programming skills.</li>
          <li>You do need financial logic — understanding credit, risk, payments, and regulatory frameworks is what makes commerce graduates useful here.</li>
          <li>Excel and basic data comfort go further than most students expect in FinTech entry roles.</li>
          <li>Python or SQL is a clear booster but not a gating requirement for every FinTech role.</li>
          <li>The fastest entry path: B.Com or BBA + a fintech internship or a digital payments / financial analytics certification.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="note-line">
      The FinTech sector in India is projected to reach $2.1 trillion by 2030. Students who understand finance and can operate in digital environments will find far more doors open than those who treat finance and technology as entirely separate worlds.
    </p>
  </section>

